WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.136
-Our next guest is an Emmy-
and Tony-nominated actress

00:00:03.136 --> 00:00:05.037
you know from shows
such as "Rectify,"

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:07.573
"Search Party," and her role
as Gerri on "Succession."

00:00:07.573 --> 00:00:10.577
She's in the cast of the new
stop-motion comedy series

00:00:10.577 --> 00:00:12.745
"In the Know," which begins
streaming on Peacock

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:13.913
January 25th.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.281
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.617
-Oh, a woman my age doesn't
have to worry about style.

00:00:17.617 --> 00:00:19.018
I always tell my hairdresser,

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:21.187
"Just try to make
my head look invisible."

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.455
-I think you look great.

00:00:23.455 --> 00:00:26.025
Oh.
-Me too.

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:29.562
I'd totally smash.
For real, Barb.

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:33.132
-[ Stutters ]
Uh...thank -- thank you.

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:35.635
-You too, Carl.
-Hey!

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:41.574
-Please welcome to the show
J. Smith-Cameron, everybody.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:44.878
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:01:00.459 --> 00:01:02.896
Hello, J. How are you?
-Hello. I'm well, thank you.

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.229
How are you?

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:06.433
-Good. I'm wonderful,
because I'm seeing you again,

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:08.301
after I saw you last week
at the Emmys.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.903
You were nominated again.
Well-deserved.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:13.173
-Lost again.
-Lost again.

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.775
Lost again, but an honor
to be nominated, yes?

00:01:15.775 --> 00:01:17.376
-Definitely.
It's an end in itself.

00:01:17.376 --> 00:01:18.845
-And it was a good group.

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:21.514
You have to -- You were in a
very good category, good group.

00:01:21.514 --> 00:01:24.984
-Yes. I mean, all those ladies
had really juicy parts,

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:27.520
and sometimes, I would just be
in like one scene an episode.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.789
So, really, I had
no business even being...

00:01:30.789 --> 00:01:32.558
-You had plenty of business
being there.

00:01:32.558 --> 00:01:34.727
I thought it was a real
testament to your work that --

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:37.564
-I thought if I, by some chance,
won, I'd be like --

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:40.833
get up there and go,
"I demand a recount."

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.802
-You would demand a recount
against your own best interest.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.036
-Yeah.

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:47.173
-It must be so special
when "Succession" wins,

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.874
especially this being
its last Emmys.

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:51.744
It must feel so great to even be
a part of a show that wins,

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.679
even though --
-Oh, definitely, definitely.

00:01:53.679 --> 00:01:55.915
It was such
a collaborative show, too.

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:57.683
So that's particularly
true in those cases.

00:01:57.683 --> 00:02:01.454
-I think one of the favorite
arcs of the show was, you know,

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.557
what happened
with Gerry and Roman.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:08.727
I mean, it was
really cool, really weird.

00:02:08.727 --> 00:02:10.963
And, certainly,
based on the first

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:12.532
few times
we saw you guys together,

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:14.233
none of us would have
predicted where it went.

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:16.602
I'm assuming the same for you.

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:20.707
-Yes, exactly. Exactly.
Yeah, it was a deeply strange.

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:22.074
-Deeply strange.

00:02:22.074 --> 00:02:25.644
Making it even deeply --
more deeply strange,

00:02:25.644 --> 00:02:27.246
you've known
Kieran Culkin forever.

00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:30.116
He'd been in plays with you.
He'd been in films with you.

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:32.418
Your husband, Ken Lonergan,
a great playwright,

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:33.953
has worked with him many times.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.355
Did that make it worse?

00:02:36.355 --> 00:02:39.425
-[ Laughs ]
No, it made it easier.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:40.626
Like, we were pals,

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:42.561
and I was used
to his snarky sense of humor.

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:45.198
And my husband has
a sarcastic sense of humor,

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:47.400
and I'm trying to learn
how to have

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.602
a more sarcastic sense of humor
and keep up with him.

00:02:50.602 --> 00:02:53.506
No, I think it came about

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:56.342
because we would sort of mock
flirt on the set all the time.

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:59.946
And that show, the writers
were so collaborative with us,

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:02.782
they would take hints
from how we behaved on set

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:03.883
and then write toward that.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.019
-Oh, my God.
So it's your fault.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.820
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.121
-It's Kieran's fault.
-It's Kieran's fault.

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:11.391
Kieran, by the way --
I love Kieran a great deal.

00:03:11.391 --> 00:03:14.327
Kieran is an agent of chaos
in real life.

00:03:14.327 --> 00:03:15.428
-Yes.

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.497
-And we actually have
a clip to prove

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:20.566
just what a little --
Like, he makes mischief.

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:23.302
And let's take a look at here
you are on the red carpet.

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:24.536
-Mom scams.

00:03:24.536 --> 00:03:27.039
-And I love Kieran Culkin,
and he's my absolute

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:29.275
favorite to work with, even
though he's an ass[bleep] to me

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:30.943
and I hate him
in our personal life.

00:03:30.943 --> 00:03:33.245
You really hate me, don't you?
-Yeah. Ooh!

00:03:33.245 --> 00:03:34.981
-You look lovely.
You smell nice.

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:38.918
-Want to kiss?
-Okay.

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:40.420
-I mean, I get...

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.488
I get if the writers
of "Succession" saw that,

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:45.858
they're like, "We gotta --
We're missing something here."

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:47.392
Here's a photo you brought us.

00:03:47.392 --> 00:03:50.262
Can you tell us what
happened to Kieran right there?

00:03:50.262 --> 00:03:55.735
-Yes. So, that was one
evening after a table read.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:59.205
And we all went to dinner,
and he was just riding me

00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.507
the whole time, like,
being horrid.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:04.710
And, like, if I couldn't
hear something that was --

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:06.445
Everyone would laugh
at something that was said

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:07.713
on the other end of the table.

00:04:07.713 --> 00:04:09.349
And I'd be like,
"What'd he say? What'd he say?"

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:10.850
He'd be like,
"Oh, I'm sorry, dear.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.752
Did you forget
your hearing aids?

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.654
Or, like, if I yawned,
he'd be like,

00:04:15.654 --> 00:04:17.122
"Is it past your bedtime?

00:04:17.122 --> 00:04:18.591
That's so terrible."

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.727
So, you know, at one point,
I just threw my martini at him.

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:23.262
-Yeah. There you go.

00:04:23.262 --> 00:04:24.363
That's why you should --
Well, within your rights.

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:25.932
-Thank you.
-Well within your rights.

00:04:27.299 --> 00:04:29.202
-He was asking for it.

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:32.204
-You are, you know,
a very integral cast member

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:33.806
on one of the biggest shows
in recent years.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.875
I would imagine you get
recognized a great deal more.

00:04:35.875 --> 00:04:37.076
I mean, you've been
onstage for years.

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:38.544
You've been in television
for years.

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:39.946
But it must be
a weird sensation.

00:04:39.946 --> 00:04:41.647
Have you noticed the shift?

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:44.116
-Yes. Yes. Somewhat, yes.
-Yeah.

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:46.318
-Sometimes, people are like,
"Oh, Team Gerri," you know.

00:04:46.318 --> 00:04:49.422
I don't really know if my name,
J. Smith-Cameron,

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:50.590
is known like --

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.957
-Gotcha. Right.
You're just Gerri to them.

00:04:51.957 --> 00:04:53.493
Yes, okay.

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:57.062
-Like, I met the maître d' at a
popular restaurant recently,

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:58.664
and they said, "You know,
if you want to come in,

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:01.100
just text me,
and I'll put a word in.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.402
You know,
I'll contact the maître d',

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:05.437
the person on the desk."

00:05:05.437 --> 00:05:07.106
-And I tried that.
-Yeah.

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:10.309
-And I got there
before his text had come in,

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:11.343
and I was like, "Hi.

00:05:11.343 --> 00:05:15.147
I'm J. Smith-Cameron."

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.183
And they were like, "Uh-huh.
Do you have a reservation?"

00:05:18.183 --> 00:05:19.552
I was like, "No, no,
but, you know,

00:05:19.552 --> 00:05:20.686
Courtney said he would call."

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:21.821
You know, nothing.

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:23.389
And it reminded me
of this old joke.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:26.059
I don't know if you've ever
heard it, but, you know, okay,

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:29.228
the joke goes
a quasi-famous actress --

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:32.231
let's say her name
is J. Smith-Cameron --

00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:33.833
is in a restaurant,

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:36.935
and, you know, the waiter brings
her the wrong dish or something

00:05:36.935 --> 00:05:38.537
and is kind of snooty about it.

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:42.408
So J. Smith-Cameron goes,
"Do you know who I am?"

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:46.111
And the waiter says,
"Give me a hint."

00:05:46.111 --> 00:05:50.516
And then the actress says,
"I'm J. Smith-Cameron."

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:52.585
And the waiter says,
"Give me another hint."

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:55.321
[ Laughter ]

00:05:55.321 --> 00:06:00.092
-Now, before you had
this recognizable face and name,

00:06:00.092 --> 00:06:02.961
you used to sneak into
the Plaza Hotel.

00:06:02.961 --> 00:06:05.664
This was back in your --
before auditions.

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.200
You were living in
Washington Heights at the time.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.668
-That's right.
-How would you find your way

00:06:08.668 --> 00:06:10.769
to sneaking into the Plaza?

00:06:10.769 --> 00:06:12.972
-Okay, yes, well, so,
I lived in Washington Heights,

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:15.074
which is, you know,
very far from Midtown,

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:16.942
where all the auditions
seemed to be.

00:06:16.942 --> 00:06:20.913
And I just developed a sort of
Holly Golightly kind of routine

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:24.349
where I found that the restrooms
at the Plaza Hotel,

00:06:24.349 --> 00:06:27.419
which was then still a hotel --

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:29.822
they had a wonderful woman
working there,

00:06:29.822 --> 00:06:32.125
and there would be, like,
cosmetics laid out.

00:06:32.125 --> 00:06:34.427
And you could wash your face.
You could brush your teeth.

00:06:34.427 --> 00:06:37.463
And, you know, I'd tip her and
I'd have a little chat with her.

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:39.498
And then I would go to
Bergdorf Goodman

00:06:39.498 --> 00:06:42.434
and put on makeup.

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:44.837
And then I was kind of
camera-ready for my --

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:46.238
-Oh, very well-played.
-Yeah.

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:47.606
-Very well played.
-Yeah.

00:06:47.606 --> 00:06:49.608
And I knew which
hotel lobbies were friendly.

00:06:49.608 --> 00:06:51.210
You could go in and look
at your sides.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.279
-Oh! Well, there. Look at that.
-Yeah.

00:06:53.279 --> 00:06:55.747
-You have a daughter,
21 years old,

00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:58.417
and I'm wondering --
who's also, I should note,

00:06:58.417 --> 00:07:00.118
has known, then,
Kieran her whole life.

00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:01.453
That's exactly right.

00:07:01.453 --> 00:07:03.889
-Does that make
"Succession" hard to watch?

00:07:03.889 --> 00:07:06.592
-She refuses to watch it.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:09.261
When she heard about that
relationship, she was like,

00:07:09.261 --> 00:07:11.097
"Mom --" Like, she got home
from school that day.

00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:14.801
She was like, "Mom,
is what I heard true?"

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:17.336
And I was like,
"Well, I think so."

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:18.404
And she went like,
"How could you?

00:07:18.404 --> 00:07:19.872
Why would you do that?"

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:22.641
And I was like, "Well, you know,
I didn't single-handedly do it.

00:07:22.641 --> 00:07:23.776
You know, it's written
into the story."

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:25.044
But she was like --

00:07:25.044 --> 00:07:26.278
-And one would say
Kieran single-handedly did it.

00:07:26.278 --> 00:07:28.080
-Yes, yes. I know. By the way.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.882
And, so -- But she was,
you know, she's not --

00:07:30.882 --> 00:07:32.184
she's a really sweet kid.

00:07:32.184 --> 00:07:34.920
Like, she's a good kid,
but she's a really fun,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.422
witty sense of humor.

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:41.160
But, you know, when they're
teenagers or middle-school age,

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:42.661
they get so embarrassed
all the time.

00:07:42.661 --> 00:07:48.100
Like, I remember trying to wake
her up for middle school

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.069
and I'm trying to wake myself up
to walk into middle school,

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:53.338
and I was blasting
Michael Jackson

00:07:53.338 --> 00:07:54.740
and singing along with it.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:58.510
And she was just like,
"Mom, cut that out.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:00.612
You're so embarrassing."

00:08:00.612 --> 00:08:05.650
And I was like, "Nellie,
we're alone in the apartment."

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:10.289
And she said, "Mom, that's how
embarrassing you are."

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:12.191
-I had a sweeter version
of the same thing.

00:08:12.191 --> 00:08:13.659
My son and I were dancing
the other day,

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:18.130
and he goes, "I love
when you dance at home."

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:19.431
And he goes, "But I don't think

00:08:19.431 --> 00:08:20.766
you should do it
out of the house."

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:27.573
That was a very funny clip
of this beautiful animation,

00:08:27.573 --> 00:08:28.640
gorgeous animation.

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:29.809
-They're puppets.

00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:32.144
-And you play a producer
on an NPR-style show.

00:08:32.144 --> 00:08:34.613
Exactly, exactly.
-And did you enjoy --

00:08:34.613 --> 00:08:35.881
That must have been nice to --

00:08:35.881 --> 00:08:37.683
I mean, considering
all the travel

00:08:37.683 --> 00:08:39.017
and the locations
of "Succession,"

00:08:39.017 --> 00:08:40.552
it must have been nice
just to go into a sound booth.

00:08:40.552 --> 00:08:42.821
-Yes, it was. It was great.
It was a really good group.

00:08:42.821 --> 00:08:45.091
Also very collaborative
kind of team.

00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:46.358
-Yeah.

00:08:46.358 --> 00:08:49.661
-My character is sort of
the straight man, in a way,

00:08:49.661 --> 00:08:51.463
although she says funny things.

00:08:51.463 --> 00:08:54.767
She's the producer
of the NPR show

00:08:54.767 --> 00:08:57.936
and sort of the
den mother of them.

00:08:57.936 --> 00:09:00.239
-Well, it's very funny.
I highly recommend it.

00:09:00.239 --> 00:09:01.840
And it's just been
so lovely having you here, J.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.043
Thanks for coming.
Come again soon.

00:09:04.043 --> 00:09:05.211
J. Smith-Cameron, everybody.

00:09:05.211 --> 00:09:07.613
"In the Know"
begins streaming on Peacock

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:08.748
January 25th.

00:09:08.748 --> 00:09:09.981
It's very funny.

00:09:09.981 --> 00:09:12.018
We'll be right back with music
from Baby Tate.

